1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,409
Come sit with me,

2
00:00:01,409 --> 00:00:02,321
sweetheart.

3
00:00:02,501 --> 00:00:04,270
No, bring the blanket,

4
00:00:04,270 --> 00:00:05,717
it's cold tonight.

5
00:00:05,897 --> 00:00:08,231
You asked me the other day what

6
00:00:08,231 --> 00:00:10,264
it was like when I was your

7
00:00:10,264 --> 00:00:10,565
age.

8
00:00:10,745 --> 00:00:13,019
Well, I was twelve years old in

9
00:00:13,019 --> 00:00:16,542
nineteen fifty-six, and I've been thinking about

10
00:00:16,542 --> 00:00:18,303
it ever since you asked.

11
00:00:18,483 --> 00:00:19,771
So let me tell you.

12
00:00:19,951 --> 00:00:20,799
All of it.

13
00:00:21,148 --> 00:00:23,719
My name was Ruth Ann Miller back

14
00:00:23,719 --> 00:00:24,121
then.

15
00:00:24,301 --> 00:00:26,495
I lived on a street of white

16
00:00:26,495 --> 00:00:28,063
houses in Mansfield,

17
00:00:28,063 --> 00:00:29,709
Ohio, with my mother,

18
00:00:29,709 --> 00:00:32,453
my father, my little brother Danny,

19
00:00:32,453 --> 00:00:34,883
and a dog named Skipper who was

20
00:00:34,883 --> 00:00:36,607
afraid of the mailman.

21
00:00:36,787 --> 00:00:39,736
Nobody locked their doors on our street.

22
00:00:39,916 --> 00:00:42,296
I'm not saying that to sound old.

23
00:00:42,476 --> 00:00:44,078
It's just true.

24
00:00:44,428 --> 00:00:46,406
Your world is so big,

25
00:00:46,406 --> 00:00:46,971
honey.

26
00:00:47,151 --> 00:00:49,281
You can see the whole planet on

27
00:00:49,281 --> 00:00:50,518
that little phone.

28
00:00:50,698 --> 00:00:53,542
My world was about six blocks wide.

29
00:00:53,722 --> 00:00:55,545
But I'll tell you a secret.

30
00:00:55,725 --> 00:00:57,629
It didn't feel small.

31
00:00:57,809 --> 00:01:00,140
It felt like it had everything in

32
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:00,352
it.

33
00:01:00,532 --> 00:01:02,587
Let me show you what I mean.

34
00:01:02,936 --> 00:01:05,746
My mornings started with the milkman.

35
00:01:05,926 --> 00:01:07,992
You've never heard that sound,

36
00:01:07,992 --> 00:01:10,884
glass bottles clinking on the porch before

37
00:01:10,884 --> 00:01:11,917
the sun was up.

38
00:01:12,097 --> 00:01:14,304
Mother left a note in the empty

39
00:01:14,304 --> 00:01:14,802
bottle.

40
00:01:14,982 --> 00:01:17,700
Two quarts and a pint of cream,

41
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:18,314
please.

42
00:01:18,494 --> 00:01:20,842
In winter the cream froze and pushed

43
00:01:20,842 --> 00:01:22,994
the little paper cap right up out

44
00:01:22,994 --> 00:01:23,907
of the bottle,

45
00:01:23,907 --> 00:01:24,624
like a hat.

46
00:01:24,974 --> 00:01:27,692
Mother was already in the kitchen when

47
00:01:27,692 --> 00:01:28,550
I came down.

48
00:01:28,730 --> 00:01:30,909
She wore an apron every day of

49
00:01:30,909 --> 00:01:31,563
her life.

50
00:01:31,743 --> 00:01:34,242
Breakfast was oatmeal or eggs,

51
00:01:34,242 --> 00:01:36,157
toast with real butter,

52
00:01:36,157 --> 00:01:39,073
and orange juice made from a frozen

53
00:01:39,073 --> 00:01:39,406
can.

54
00:01:39,586 --> 00:01:42,471
Father read the Mansfield News Journal at

55
00:01:42,471 --> 00:01:45,074
the table and drank his coffee black,

56
00:01:45,074 --> 00:01:46,974
and you did not talk to him

57
00:01:46,974 --> 00:01:48,874
until he folded that paper.

58
00:01:49,223 --> 00:01:50,965
Then I walked to school.

59
00:01:51,145 --> 00:01:53,003
Walked, sweetheart.

60
00:01:53,183 --> 00:01:54,878
Nobody drove us.

61
00:01:55,058 --> 00:01:57,048
Danny and I went together,

62
00:01:57,048 --> 00:01:59,727
and we picked up the Kowalski girls

63
00:01:59,727 --> 00:02:00,799
on the corner,

64
00:02:00,799 --> 00:02:02,713
and by the time we got to

65
00:02:02,713 --> 00:02:05,392
the school there were twenty of us,

66
00:02:05,392 --> 00:02:07,306
swinging our lunch pails.

67
00:02:07,486 --> 00:02:08,633
Mine was tin,

68
00:02:08,633 --> 00:02:11,016
with Roy Rogers on the lid.

69
00:02:11,196 --> 00:02:13,714
I loved that lunch pail more than

70
00:02:13,714 --> 00:02:15,317
anything I own today.

71
00:02:15,667 --> 00:02:16,968
School was strict.

72
00:02:17,148 --> 00:02:19,300
We stood every morning and said the

73
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:21,638
Pledge of Allegiance with our hands on

74
00:02:21,638 --> 00:02:22,314
our hearts.

75
00:02:22,494 --> 00:02:23,745
Two years before,

76
00:02:23,745 --> 00:02:26,614
Congress had added the words under God,

77
00:02:26,614 --> 00:02:29,262
and some of the older teachers still

78
00:02:29,262 --> 00:02:30,145
forgot them.

79
00:02:30,325 --> 00:02:32,562
The boys wore their hair short and

80
00:02:32,562 --> 00:02:34,075
the girls wore dresses.

81
00:02:34,255 --> 00:02:35,857
Always dresses.

82
00:02:36,037 --> 00:02:37,441
Even in January,

83
00:02:37,441 --> 00:02:39,811
with snow up to your knees.

84
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:42,645
My teacher that year was Mrs.

85
00:02:42,825 --> 00:02:43,800
Hadley.

86
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:45,938
She had gray hair in a bun

87
00:02:45,938 --> 00:02:47,369
and a wooden ruler,

88
00:02:47,369 --> 00:02:49,553
and she knew how to use both.

89
00:02:49,733 --> 00:02:52,623
We wrote with fountain pens and blotted

90
00:02:52,623 --> 00:02:53,216
the ink.

91
00:02:53,396 --> 00:02:55,169
If you talked in class,

92
00:02:55,169 --> 00:02:57,018
you stood in the corner.

93
00:02:57,198 --> 00:02:58,486
If you chewed gum,

94
00:02:58,486 --> 00:03:00,275
you wore it on your nose.

95
00:03:00,455 --> 00:03:02,324
I only did that once.

96
00:03:02,674 --> 00:03:04,497
And then there were the drills.

97
00:03:04,677 --> 00:03:06,494
A bell would ring,

98
00:03:06,494 --> 00:03:07,301
and Mrs.

99
00:03:07,481 --> 00:03:09,054
Hadley would say,

100
00:03:09,054 --> 00:03:10,442
duck and cover.

101
00:03:10,622 --> 00:03:13,355
We crawled under our wooden desks and

102
00:03:13,355 --> 00:03:15,498
put our hands over our heads.

103
00:03:15,678 --> 00:03:18,515
We were practicing for an atomic bomb,

104
00:03:18,515 --> 00:03:18,963
honey.

105
00:03:19,143 --> 00:03:20,351
Twelve years old.

106
00:03:20,531 --> 00:03:22,981
We didn't really understand it.

107
00:03:23,161 --> 00:03:24,342
We just did it,

108
00:03:24,342 --> 00:03:27,178
and then we went back to arithmetic.

109
00:03:27,527 --> 00:03:29,765
That year we also lined up in

110
00:03:29,765 --> 00:03:31,231
the gym for a shot.

111
00:03:31,411 --> 00:03:33,176
The polio vaccine.

112
00:03:33,356 --> 00:03:35,874
You don't know how afraid our parents

113
00:03:35,874 --> 00:03:36,827
were of polio.

114
00:03:37,007 --> 00:03:39,676
Every summer, children got sick,

115
00:03:39,676 --> 00:03:42,011
and some never walked again.

116
00:03:42,191 --> 00:03:44,901
When Doctor Salk's vaccine came,

117
00:03:44,901 --> 00:03:47,102
mothers cried in that gym.

118
00:03:47,282 --> 00:03:49,582
I remember mine holding my hand so

119
00:03:49,582 --> 00:03:51,949
tight it hurt more than the needle.

120
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:55,630
Now you always ask me about money,

121
00:03:55,630 --> 00:03:57,002
so here it is.

122
00:03:57,182 --> 00:03:59,747
A bottle of Coca-Cola was a nickel.

123
00:03:59,927 --> 00:04:02,087
A comic book was a dime.

124
00:04:02,267 --> 00:04:04,844
A loaf of bread was about eighteen

125
00:04:04,844 --> 00:04:06,967
cents, and a stamp to mail a

126
00:04:06,967 --> 00:04:08,710
letter was three cents.

127
00:04:08,890 --> 00:04:12,075
Gasoline was about twenty-two cents a gallon,

128
00:04:12,075 --> 00:04:14,127
and a man came out and pumped

129
00:04:14,127 --> 00:04:16,746
it for you and washed your windshield

130
00:04:16,746 --> 00:04:17,029
too.

131
00:04:17,379 --> 00:04:20,682
My father worked at the Westinghouse plant,

132
00:04:20,682 --> 00:04:23,601
making electric ranges and appliances.

133
00:04:23,781 --> 00:04:26,466
He made about eighty dollars a week,

134
00:04:26,466 --> 00:04:28,777
and on that one paycheck we had

135
00:04:28,777 --> 00:04:29,896
a house, a car,

136
00:04:29,896 --> 00:04:31,238
food on the table,

137
00:04:31,238 --> 00:04:33,325
and a week at the lake every

138
00:04:33,325 --> 00:04:33,847
August.

139
00:04:34,027 --> 00:04:36,825
Mother didn't work outside the house.

140
00:04:37,005 --> 00:04:39,838
Hardly any mothers on our street did.

141
00:04:40,018 --> 00:04:42,387
That was just how it was.

142
00:04:42,737 --> 00:04:45,706
My allowance was twenty-five cents a week,

143
00:04:45,706 --> 00:04:47,969
if I dried the dishes and dusted

144
00:04:47,969 --> 00:04:49,029
the front room.

145
00:04:49,209 --> 00:04:52,042
Twenty-five cents, sweetheart.

146
00:04:52,222 --> 00:04:53,852
And I'll tell you,

147
00:04:53,852 --> 00:04:54,939
I felt rich.

148
00:04:55,119 --> 00:04:57,604
Because a quarter could get you into

149
00:04:57,604 --> 00:04:58,915
the Saturday movie,

150
00:04:58,915 --> 00:05:00,088
a bag of popcorn,

151
00:05:00,088 --> 00:05:02,159
and still leave a penny or two

152
00:05:02,159 --> 00:05:03,954
for candy on the way home.

153
00:05:04,303 --> 00:05:06,513
Saturday was the best day of the

154
00:05:06,513 --> 00:05:06,858
week.

155
00:05:07,038 --> 00:05:09,615
In the morning we watched television,

156
00:05:09,615 --> 00:05:11,775
but only if we had finished our

157
00:05:11,775 --> 00:05:12,262
chores.

158
00:05:12,442 --> 00:05:13,650
We had one set,

159
00:05:13,650 --> 00:05:16,064
a big wooden box with a little

160
00:05:16,064 --> 00:05:18,479
round screen, black and white.

161
00:05:18,659 --> 00:05:20,794
Howdy Doody, Mighty Mouse,

162
00:05:20,794 --> 00:05:22,108
The Lone Ranger.

163
00:05:22,288 --> 00:05:24,163
And when the shows ended,

164
00:05:24,163 --> 00:05:26,564
there was a test pattern with an

165
00:05:26,564 --> 00:05:27,915
Indian head on it,

166
00:05:27,915 --> 00:05:29,265
and that was that.

167
00:05:29,616 --> 00:05:31,786
In the afternoon we went to the

168
00:05:31,786 --> 00:05:34,027
matinee at the theater downtown.

169
00:05:34,207 --> 00:05:36,177
Two cartoons, a newsreel,

170
00:05:36,177 --> 00:05:38,935
a serial that always ended with the

171
00:05:38,935 --> 00:05:41,377
hero about to fall off a cliff,

172
00:05:41,377 --> 00:05:43,426
and then the main picture.

173
00:05:43,606 --> 00:05:45,339
Hundreds of children,

174
00:05:45,339 --> 00:05:48,064
no grownups, everybody screaming.

175
00:05:48,244 --> 00:05:50,451
The poor usher walked up and down

176
00:05:50,451 --> 00:05:52,458
the aisle with his flashlight,

177
00:05:52,458 --> 00:05:54,397
and nobody paid him any mind.

178
00:05:54,748 --> 00:05:56,751
On the way home we stopped at

179
00:05:56,751 --> 00:05:57,580
Woolworth's.

180
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:00,478
Oh, honey, I wish you could have

181
00:06:00,478 --> 00:06:02,091
smelled that store.

182
00:06:02,271 --> 00:06:04,840
Popcorn, roasted peanuts,

183
00:06:04,840 --> 00:06:07,101
perfume and floor wax.

184
00:06:07,281 --> 00:06:09,579
Birds in cages at the back.

185
00:06:09,759 --> 00:06:11,420
Goldfish in tanks.

186
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:14,510
Long counters of everything a girl could

187
00:06:14,510 --> 00:06:17,056
want, and a lunch counter where you

188
00:06:17,056 --> 00:06:19,602
sat on a spinning stool and ordered

189
00:06:19,602 --> 00:06:20,621
a cherry Coke.

190
00:06:20,971 --> 00:06:22,468
And the penny candy.

191
00:06:22,648 --> 00:06:25,179
Candy buttons on paper strips.

192
00:06:25,359 --> 00:06:26,648
Wax lips.

193
00:06:26,828 --> 00:06:28,233
Mary Janes.

194
00:06:28,413 --> 00:06:31,347
Bazooka bubble gum with the little comic

195
00:06:31,347 --> 00:06:31,861
inside.

196
00:06:32,041 --> 00:06:33,923
And candy cigarettes,

197
00:06:33,923 --> 00:06:34,909
sweetheart.

198
00:06:35,089 --> 00:06:37,685
Little white sticks with red tips,

199
00:06:37,685 --> 00:06:39,975
in a box that looked just like

200
00:06:39,975 --> 00:06:41,044
the real ones.

201
00:06:41,224 --> 00:06:43,749
We'd pretend to smoke them like our

202
00:06:43,749 --> 00:06:44,615
fathers did.

203
00:06:44,795 --> 00:06:46,815
Nobody thought a thing about it.

204
00:06:47,165 --> 00:06:49,603
That summer, we lived outside.

205
00:06:49,783 --> 00:06:52,411
We left after breakfast and came home

206
00:06:52,411 --> 00:06:54,543
when the streetlights came on.

207
00:06:54,723 --> 00:06:56,936
Nobody knew where we were,

208
00:06:56,936 --> 00:06:58,554
and nobody worried.

209
00:06:58,734 --> 00:07:01,555
We rode our bicycles everywhere.

210
00:07:01,735 --> 00:07:03,871
Mine was a blue Schwinn I bought

211
00:07:03,871 --> 00:07:06,475
with birthday money from Grandma Pearl,

212
00:07:06,475 --> 00:07:08,478
and I put playing cards in the

213
00:07:08,478 --> 00:07:11,081
spokes so it sounded like a motorcycle.

214
00:07:11,432 --> 00:07:13,826
We played kick the can and red

215
00:07:13,826 --> 00:07:16,221
rover until it was too dark to

216
00:07:16,221 --> 00:07:16,540
see.

217
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:19,122
We caught lightning bugs in a Mason

218
00:07:19,122 --> 00:07:21,317
jar with holes poked in the lid.

219
00:07:21,497 --> 00:07:24,481
We drank water straight from the garden

220
00:07:24,481 --> 00:07:27,082
hose, warm at first and then cold.

221
00:07:27,262 --> 00:07:29,792
And when the ice cream truck came

222
00:07:29,792 --> 00:07:31,709
jingling down the street,

223
00:07:31,709 --> 00:07:34,239
every door on the block opened at

224
00:07:34,239 --> 00:07:34,623
once.

225
00:07:34,973 --> 00:07:37,794
In August, we drove to Lake Erie.

226
00:07:37,974 --> 00:07:40,331
Father packed the trunk of our green

227
00:07:40,331 --> 00:07:42,426
Chevrolet so tight he had to sit

228
00:07:42,426 --> 00:07:43,605
on it to close it.

229
00:07:43,785 --> 00:07:45,123
No seat belts,

230
00:07:45,123 --> 00:07:47,035
no air conditioning.

231
00:07:47,215 --> 00:07:49,469
Danny and I fought in the back

232
00:07:49,469 --> 00:07:50,896
seat the whole way,

233
00:07:50,896 --> 00:07:52,098
and Mother said,

234
00:07:52,098 --> 00:07:54,728
don't make me turn this car around,

235
00:07:54,728 --> 00:07:56,155
at least ten times.

236
00:07:56,335 --> 00:07:58,750
She never once turned it around.

237
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:00,405
That same summer,

238
00:08:00,405 --> 00:08:03,630
President Eisenhower signed a law to build

239
00:08:03,630 --> 00:08:05,473
the interstate highways.

240
00:08:05,653 --> 00:08:08,185
Father read about it at the breakfast

241
00:08:08,185 --> 00:08:09,211
table and said,

242
00:08:09,211 --> 00:08:12,016
Ruthie, someday you'll drive from here to

243
00:08:12,016 --> 00:08:14,616
California without a single stoplight.

244
00:08:14,796 --> 00:08:16,131
I laughed at him.

245
00:08:16,311 --> 00:08:17,809
I didn't believe it.

246
00:08:17,989 --> 00:08:19,203
He was right,

247
00:08:19,203 --> 00:08:20,137
of course.

248
00:08:20,317 --> 00:08:21,614
He usually was,

249
00:08:21,614 --> 00:08:24,555
though I'd never have told him so.

250
00:08:24,904 --> 00:08:26,913
Now, September.

251
00:08:27,093 --> 00:08:29,763
I have to tell you about September.

252
00:08:29,943 --> 00:08:32,022
On the ninth of September,

253
00:08:32,022 --> 00:08:35,620
nineteen fifty-six, Elvis Presley sang on the

254
00:08:35,620 --> 00:08:36,979
Ed Sullivan Show.

255
00:08:37,159 --> 00:08:40,758
About sixty million Americans watched that night.

256
00:08:40,938 --> 00:08:43,280
More than four out of every five

257
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:46,428
televisions in the whole country were tuned

258
00:08:46,428 --> 00:08:47,672
to that one show.

259
00:08:48,021 --> 00:08:49,624
I begged to stay up.

260
00:08:49,804 --> 00:08:52,000
Father said that boy was a bad

261
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,490
influence, and he sat in his chair

262
00:08:54,490 --> 00:08:57,199
with his arms crossed the whole time.

263
00:08:57,379 --> 00:09:00,044
But when Elvis started to sing Hound

264
00:09:00,044 --> 00:09:02,116
Dog, I saw his foot tapping.

265
00:09:02,296 --> 00:09:03,231
I saw it,

266
00:09:03,231 --> 00:09:04,374
sweetheart.

267
00:09:04,554 --> 00:09:07,338
I told your grandfather that story at

268
00:09:07,338 --> 00:09:10,047
our wedding, and he laughed until he

269
00:09:10,047 --> 00:09:10,499
cried.

270
00:09:10,848 --> 00:09:13,722
After that, every girl at school wanted

271
00:09:13,722 --> 00:09:14,900
a record player.

272
00:09:15,080 --> 00:09:17,349
I saved for months and bought my

273
00:09:17,349 --> 00:09:19,759
first record at the five and dime.

274
00:09:19,939 --> 00:09:22,869
Forty-five rpm, with a big hole in

275
00:09:22,869 --> 00:09:23,817
the middle.

276
00:09:23,997 --> 00:09:26,324
I played it until it was all

277
00:09:26,324 --> 00:09:27,155
scratches.

278
00:09:27,335 --> 00:09:29,993
My mother said she'd hear that song

279
00:09:29,993 --> 00:09:30,980
in her sleep.

280
00:09:31,160 --> 00:09:33,761
I think she secretly liked it too.

281
00:09:34,110 --> 00:09:36,026
Our telephone was black,

282
00:09:36,026 --> 00:09:38,500
and it was heavy enough to hurt

283
00:09:38,500 --> 00:09:39,219
somebody.

284
00:09:39,399 --> 00:09:41,436
It sat on a little table in

285
00:09:41,436 --> 00:09:42,116
the hall.

286
00:09:42,296 --> 00:09:44,602
And we shared the line with three

287
00:09:44,602 --> 00:09:45,651
other families.

288
00:09:45,831 --> 00:09:46,807
A party line,

289
00:09:46,807 --> 00:09:47,932
they called it.

290
00:09:48,112 --> 00:09:50,290
You'd pick it up to call your

291
00:09:50,290 --> 00:09:51,491
friend, and Mrs.

292
00:09:51,671 --> 00:09:54,711
Duncan from down the street would already

293
00:09:54,711 --> 00:09:55,379
be on it,

294
00:09:55,379 --> 00:09:57,603
talking about her gallbladder.

295
00:09:57,953 --> 00:10:00,717
Mother cooked everything from scratch.

296
00:10:00,897 --> 00:10:02,693
Pot roast on Sunday,

297
00:10:02,693 --> 00:10:04,669
meatloaf on Wednesday,

298
00:10:04,669 --> 00:10:06,017
fish on Friday.

299
00:10:06,197 --> 00:10:09,005
Jell-O salad with little pieces of fruit

300
00:10:09,005 --> 00:10:09,847
stuck in it,

301
00:10:09,847 --> 00:10:10,690
for company.

302
00:10:10,870 --> 00:10:13,145
Nothing was ever thrown away.

303
00:10:13,325 --> 00:10:15,455
Leftover roast became hash,

304
00:10:15,455 --> 00:10:17,584
stale bread became pudding,

305
00:10:17,584 --> 00:10:20,266
and bacon grease lived in a coffee

306
00:10:20,266 --> 00:10:22,001
can next to the stove,

307
00:10:22,001 --> 00:10:22,869
for frying.

308
00:10:23,218 --> 00:10:25,256
Every night at six o'clock,

309
00:10:25,256 --> 00:10:27,595
we sat down to supper together.

310
00:10:27,775 --> 00:10:29,355
All four of us,

311
00:10:29,355 --> 00:10:30,829
no exceptions.

312
00:10:31,009 --> 00:10:33,517
You didn't leave the table until you

313
00:10:33,517 --> 00:10:34,422
were excused.

314
00:10:34,602 --> 00:10:36,704
You ate what was on your plate,

315
00:10:36,704 --> 00:10:38,805
or you sat there looking at it.

316
00:10:38,985 --> 00:10:40,906
I sat with a plate of liver

317
00:10:40,906 --> 00:10:43,397
and onions until eight thirty once.

318
00:10:43,577 --> 00:10:45,592
I still won't eat liver,

319
00:10:45,592 --> 00:10:46,096
honey.

320
00:10:46,276 --> 00:10:47,623
Not for anybody.

321
00:10:47,972 --> 00:10:49,192
And on Sundays,

322
00:10:49,192 --> 00:10:51,630
we put on our best clothes and

323
00:10:51,630 --> 00:10:52,849
went to church.

324
00:10:53,029 --> 00:10:55,318
White gloves, a little hat,

325
00:10:55,318 --> 00:10:56,930
shoes that pinched.

326
00:10:57,110 --> 00:10:59,373
Then dinner at Grandma Pearl's,

327
00:10:59,373 --> 00:11:01,783
with the good china and the whole

328
00:11:01,783 --> 00:11:03,901
family around one long table,

329
00:11:03,901 --> 00:11:07,260
uncles arguing about the Cleveland Indians and

330
00:11:07,260 --> 00:11:09,962
aunts trading recipes in the kitchen.

331
00:11:10,311 --> 00:11:12,834
In November there was an election,

332
00:11:12,834 --> 00:11:14,688
and Eisenhower won again.

333
00:11:14,868 --> 00:11:17,121
Ike, everybody called him.

334
00:11:17,301 --> 00:11:19,578
Father wore an I Like Ike button

335
00:11:19,578 --> 00:11:21,144
on his coat for weeks.

336
00:11:21,324 --> 00:11:23,792
At school we held our own pretend

337
00:11:23,792 --> 00:11:26,560
vote with paper ballots in a shoebox.

338
00:11:26,740 --> 00:11:28,133
Ike won there too,

339
00:11:28,133 --> 00:11:28,830
by a lot.

340
00:11:29,010 --> 00:11:31,450
I voted for him because I liked

341
00:11:31,450 --> 00:11:32,237
his smile.

342
00:11:32,417 --> 00:11:34,797
That's how twelve-year-olds vote.

343
00:11:35,147 --> 00:11:38,085
Thanksgiving was at our house that year.

344
00:11:38,265 --> 00:11:40,038
Mother got up at five in the

345
00:11:40,038 --> 00:11:41,876
morning to put the turkey in.

346
00:11:42,056 --> 00:11:43,658
Twenty-two pounds.

347
00:11:43,838 --> 00:11:45,715
We had mashed potatoes,

348
00:11:45,715 --> 00:11:49,550
sweet potatoes with little marshmallows on top,

349
00:11:49,550 --> 00:11:52,406
green beans and three kinds of pie.

350
00:11:52,586 --> 00:11:54,794
Then the men fell asleep in front

351
00:11:54,794 --> 00:11:56,199
of the football game,

352
00:11:56,199 --> 00:11:58,541
and the women did every single dish

353
00:11:58,541 --> 00:11:59,076
by hand.

354
00:11:59,425 --> 00:12:00,656
And Christmas.

355
00:12:00,836 --> 00:12:02,520
Oh, sweetheart.

356
00:12:02,700 --> 00:12:05,183
The Sears catalog came in the fall,

357
00:12:05,183 --> 00:12:07,454
the one we called the Wish Book,

358
00:12:07,454 --> 00:12:10,080
and Danny and I circled everything in

359
00:12:10,080 --> 00:12:10,293
it.

360
00:12:10,473 --> 00:12:12,736
I wanted a doll with real hair

361
00:12:12,736 --> 00:12:13,491
that year.

362
00:12:13,671 --> 00:12:16,222
On Christmas morning she was under the

363
00:12:16,222 --> 00:12:18,303
tree, and I named her Margaret.

364
00:12:18,483 --> 00:12:19,912
I still have her.

365
00:12:20,092 --> 00:12:22,704
She's in the cedar chest upstairs.

366
00:12:22,884 --> 00:12:24,753
I'll show you tomorrow.

367
00:12:25,102 --> 00:12:27,327
Now, I don't want you to think

368
00:12:27,327 --> 00:12:28,736
it was all perfect.

369
00:12:28,916 --> 00:12:29,799
It wasn't.

370
00:12:29,979 --> 00:12:33,386
Not everybody was welcome on every street,

371
00:12:33,386 --> 00:12:35,495
or at every lunch counter,

372
00:12:35,495 --> 00:12:36,631
the way I was.

373
00:12:36,811 --> 00:12:39,490
Women couldn't do half the things you'll

374
00:12:39,490 --> 00:12:39,691
do.

375
00:12:39,871 --> 00:12:41,920
And we didn't know half of what

376
00:12:41,920 --> 00:12:44,433
we know now about cigarettes or bombs.

377
00:12:44,613 --> 00:12:46,303
Some things got better,

378
00:12:46,303 --> 00:12:48,212
honey, and they needed to.

379
00:12:48,562 --> 00:12:50,722
But you asked me what I miss.

380
00:12:50,902 --> 00:12:53,071
I miss the sound of the screen

381
00:12:53,071 --> 00:12:53,433
door.

382
00:12:53,613 --> 00:12:55,941
I miss knowing every family on the

383
00:12:55,941 --> 00:12:56,968
street by name.

384
00:12:57,148 --> 00:12:59,247
I miss being bored on a summer

385
00:12:59,247 --> 00:13:02,114
afternoon, lying in the grass and looking

386
00:13:02,114 --> 00:13:04,353
at the clouds with nothing to do

387
00:13:04,353 --> 00:13:05,612
and nowhere to be.

388
00:13:05,792 --> 00:13:08,110
I don't think children get to be

389
00:13:08,110 --> 00:13:09,124
bored anymore.

390
00:13:09,304 --> 00:13:11,289
I think that's a loss.

391
00:13:11,640 --> 00:13:12,481
Most of all,

392
00:13:12,481 --> 00:13:14,654
I miss my mother's kitchen on a

393
00:13:14,654 --> 00:13:17,249
Saturday night, with the radio on and

394
00:13:17,249 --> 00:13:19,352
the smell of something baking,

395
00:13:19,352 --> 00:13:22,017
and my father laughing at something on

396
00:13:22,017 --> 00:13:24,751
the television, and Danny asleep on the

397
00:13:24,751 --> 00:13:25,943
rug with Skipper.

398
00:13:26,123 --> 00:13:27,810
I didn't know it then,

399
00:13:27,810 --> 00:13:28,654
sweetheart.

400
00:13:28,834 --> 00:13:31,215
I didn't know those were the good

401
00:13:31,215 --> 00:13:31,864
old days.

402
00:13:32,044 --> 00:13:33,283
Nobody ever does,

403
00:13:33,283 --> 00:13:35,179
while they're living them.

404
00:13:35,528 --> 00:13:36,744
Now, it's late,

405
00:13:36,744 --> 00:13:39,499
and your mother will have my hide.

406
00:13:39,679 --> 00:13:41,244
But come back tomorrow,

407
00:13:41,244 --> 00:13:43,490
and I'll tell you about the first

408
00:13:43,490 --> 00:13:44,783
time I drove a car,

409
00:13:44,783 --> 00:13:46,552
and the boy who taught me.

410
00:13:46,732 --> 00:13:48,218
Your grandfather.

411
00:13:48,398 --> 00:13:50,244
Goodnight, sweetheart.

412
00:13:50,424 --> 00:13:53,051
And if you liked hearing about the

413
00:13:53,051 --> 00:13:54,674
old days, stay close.

414
00:13:54,854 --> 00:13:57,666
Grandma has a lot more stories to

415
00:13:57,666 --> 00:13:58,093
tell.
